From f5ce3652393bf7a6d91fe0a72759f99d5614850b Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: William Floyd <git@notmy.space>
Date: Sat, 26 Sep 2026 08:55:58 +0000
Subject: software_isp: Keep blown highlights out of exposure and AWB

Two defects that together made bright scenes come out overexposed with
a green cast, on the rear camera of samsung-starqltechn.

AGC could not correct a large error quickly. Its step is bounded by
maxStep regardless of how wrong the exposure is, and statistics only
arrive every kStatPerNumFrames (4) frames, which caps the correction
rate at roughly 0.37 EV/s: recovering from four stops of overexposure
took some 30 updates, or ten seconds of blown preview after every open
or pan onto something bright. The five-bin MSV also saturates, so a
completely blown frame asks for much the same correction as a mildly
bright one.

Size the correction from the mean of the full 64 bin histogram while
the error is large, aiming straight at the target rather than stepping
towards it, bounded to two stops per update. The proportional term
still runs close to the target and still decides where the loop
settles, so this changes how fast a converged image is reached and not
how bright it is. Measured on a lit scene: 18 updates over 5.3s before,
3 updates after, with no overshoot.

The green cast was a consequence of that overexposure rather than a
separate fault. AWB derives its gains from ratios of the raw channel
sums, and those sums included saturated samples. Green saturates first
on a Bayer sensor, so once highlights clip sumG stops growing while
sumR and sumB keep rising, dragging the ratios towards unity and
leaving the sensor's native green excess uncorrected -- the cast
appears exactly when, and because, the highlights blow. With 53% of
samples saturated the gains collapsed from 1.72 to 1.33.

Leave saturated samples out of the sums, which means tracking the
number that contributed, as it is no longer the number of samples in
the histogram. The histogram itself still counts them, because AGC has
to see that the scene is clipping. Bail out and keep the previous gains
when too little unclipped data remains to form a ratio from. With 65%
of samples saturated the gains now come out at 1.79 against a
converged 1.72.

Together: near-clipped green pixels fall from 55.8% to 3.6%, and the
rendered G/R ratio from 1.19-1.88 across the tonal range to 0.98-1.17.

Note that sumCount is shifted like the sums it divides. Nothing that
shifts is in use here -- sumShift_ is zero for the CSI2 packed formats
-- but the black level offset was already being computed from an
unshifted count, so this is at least no worse for the unpacked 10 and
12 bit paths.

Signed-off-by: William Floyd <git@notmy.space>
